            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.15.5</h2>
<p><b>"Could not decide." should have been a task.</b> When Jarvis looked through
your Slack for what needs you, it sometimes came back with nothing at all — the
run showed the messages it had read and then stopped on "Could not decide."
Three runs in twelve ended that way.</p>

<h3>What was happening</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Jarvis answers in a fixed shape, and that shape let it hand back one
      completely blank task — no title, no reason, nothing cited. A blank task
      is not a task, so it was thrown away, and the run had nothing left to
      show.</li>
  <li>The shape now requires a title, a reason and at least one cited message
      on every task. Finding nothing is still a perfectly good answer; saying
      nothing with a blank placeholder is no longer possible.</li>
  <li>Nothing was ever lost: a run that ends this way does not count as this
      hour's look, so the next one starts fresh. It just meant an hour of
      silence that looked like a fault.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Also</h3>
<ul>
  <li>When the model behind a run is refused, the reason is now written to the
      log instead of a bare error code — so a run that quietly falls back to
      the second model can actually be explained.</li>
</ul>

<p>No change to what leaves your Mac: Slack is searched live and never stored,
and mail, meetings and notes stay local.</p>
]]></description>
